    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test, worth restating plainly before you run through them: given the actual decision rule this race will be decided by, does this specific activity move a vote that will actually change the outcome. The invisible primary and the primary-general tradeoff are the structural facts a candidate is operating under before most voters are even paying attention, deciding who is even viable long before a ballot is cast and forcing a positioning choice between two genuinely different electorates once one is. The fundamentals set the baseline the entire rest of the campaign is fighting at the margins of, a baseline every other concept in this lecture has to be measured against rather than assessed in isolation. Targeting, message and framing, and polling are how a campaign reads the race and allocates its effort and its voice within that margin, while earned, paid, and owned media are the channels that message actually has to travel through to reach anyone at all, each with its own tradeoff between control, cost, and credibility. Resource allocation applies all of that to the actual electoral map, deciding where the campaign's own money and candidate time are actually worth spending, and opposition research and rapid response defend and attack within the resulting message battle, racing to shape the same narrative before an opponent does. Get-out-the-vote closes the loop, converting everything covered before it into the only thing that actually counts on election day, a vote cast rather than a supporter merely identified. As you follow any live campaign from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order: place the candidate's invisible-primary standing, name the positioning tension they are navigating, check the fundamentals, and ask whether the tactical choices you are actually watching, an ad, a debate answer, a canvassing push, are moving a vote that matters under the race's real decision rule or simply generating activity that feels like campaigning without actually being it.",
