          "Now, turn to the leverage each side actually brings to the negotiating table once the procedural track is already set. As in any negotiation, understanding your Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement, your BATNA, what actually happens if no deal is reached at all, shapes how much you should reasonably concede at the table. A party with a strong BATNA, a genuinely good outcome even without a deal, has real leverage to hold firm on its own terms, while a party whose alternative to a deal is considerably worse has correspondingly less room to walk away from the table without paying a real cost for doing so [6]. Roger Fisher and William Ury's original framing, developed for negotiation generally rather than legislatures specifically, still applies directly here: the point of knowing your BATNA is never simply to know when to walk away, it is to know exactly how far you can credibly push before walking away actually becomes your own better option. A legislator's BATNA is rarely a single fixed number either, since it depends on a genuinely uncertain forecast of how the public, the president, and the legislator's own party would actually react if the current negotiation simply collapsed without a deal.",
